<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.13.5: Graphing Quadratic Equations</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01963</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.13.5: Graphing Quadratic Equations</md:title>
    <md:uuid>a857f36a-655e-4f98-aff6-c54fd749d355</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Cool Down (5 minutes)</title>
<title>Launch</title>
<para>Ask students to put away devices for this cool down.</para>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Consider the quadratic equation \(y=x^2-4x\). Consider the graph of the equation when answering questions a and b.</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
    <item> Where are the \(x\)-intercepts located? </item>
  </list>
  <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  <para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> The \(x\)-intercepts are at \(x=0\) and \(x=4\).</para>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
    <item> What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex? </item>
  </list>
  <para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> The correct answer is 2. The vertex is halfway between the intercepts at \(x=2\).</para>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>Here is a graph of \(y=x^2\).</item>
</list>
<para><figure><media alt="&quot;Graph of Parabola y = x squared&amp;nbsp;&quot; height=&quot;200&quot;"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/a2f1f417297e9e9347bdc79e48eb92413d81ba49"/></media></figure></para>
<para>Sketch this graph, then sketch a graph of \(y=x^2+5x\) on the same graph. Briefly explain how you know where to sketch the graph.</para>
<para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(x^2+5x\) is equivalent to \(x(x+5)\), so the \(x\)-intercepts are at \(x=0\) and \(x=-5\). Calculate the vertex by finding the \(x\)-coordinate in between \(x = -5\) and \(x = 0\) which is \(x = -2.5\). Substitute \(x=-2.5\) into the equation to find the \(y\)-coordinate: \(y=(-2.5)2+5(-2.5)=-6.188\). The vertex is at \((-2.5, -6.188)\). The squared term has a positive coefficient so the graph opens upward. Adding a positive linear term to \(x^2\) shifts the graph down and to the left.</para>
<para><figure><media alt="&quot;Graph of 2 parabolas &amp;nbsp;&quot; height=&quot;200&quot;"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/a42561305274a589ef2901336af6782c1d5656fc"/></media></figure></para>
<title>Response to Student Thinking</title>
<title>More Chances</title>
<para>This lesson goes beyond the depth of understanding required to address the standards, so there is not a need to slow down if students struggle with these ideas.</para></section>
</content>
